::SPEECH_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
A student is speaking to the principal to request permission for a school announcement. Which response uses the most appropriate voice and tone?
{
~A. Hey, can I do the announcement tomorrow? It will be quick.#This sounds casual for a principal. The message has a clear request, but the voice should be more respectful and formal for the audience.
~B. I need to make an announcement, so put me on tomorrow.#This sounds demanding. A request should respect the listener and use a tone that makes cooperation more likely.
=C. Good morning. May I have permission to make a brief announcement tomorrow?#Correct. This response matches the audience by using respectful language, a clear purpose, and a polite tone.
~D. I might say something tomorrow if that is okay or whatever.#This is unclear and too casual. Audience-aware speaking should state the purpose directly while keeping a respectful tone.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which choice best explains why audience matters when choosing tone?
{
~A. Audience matters because every speech should sound exactly the same.#This misses the main idea. Speakers adjust tone because different audiences need different levels of formality, detail, and respect.
~B. Audience matters only when the speaker is giving a long presentation.#This is too limited. Audience affects short comments, questions, discussions, and formal presentations.
~C. Audience matters because it lets the speaker avoid preparing.#This is the opposite of good communication. Knowing the audience helps the speaker prepare more carefully.
=D. Audience matters because the speaker should choose language and tone that fit the listener and purpose.#Correct. Audience awareness helps the speaker decide how formal, clear, respectful, and detailed the message should be.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which sentence uses academic vocabulary appropriately in a class presentation?
{
~A. The stuff proves the thing because it is kind of obvious.#This is too vague for academic communication. Replace unclear words like stuff and thing with precise vocabulary.
=B. The evidence supports the claim because the data show a clear pattern.#Correct. This sentence uses academic words such as evidence, claim, data, and pattern in a clear way that fits a class presentation.
~C. Everybody knows this, so I do not need to explain it.#This avoids evidence and explanation. Academic communication should use precise words and support the point clearly.
~D. My idea is right because I said it strongly.#This focuses on forceful tone instead of academic vocabulary and support. A strong presentation uses precise language and evidence.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
A student is preparing a short presentation. Which plan best shows logical organization and coherent focus?
{
=A. State the topic, explain two supporting points, and end by restating the main idea.#Correct. This plan gives the audience a clear focus, organized support, and a closing statement that reinforces the message.
~B. Share opinions in the order they come to mind without transitions.#This may confuse the audience. Logical organization uses a clear order and transitions between ideas.
~C. Start with three unrelated facts, then decide the topic at the end.#This lacks focus. A presentation should begin with a clear topic and keep each detail connected to that topic.
~D. Use a funny story and skip the evidence because the audience will remember the story.#A story can help, but it should not replace organization and support. The audience still needs a clear point and evidence.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Read the message: "Your idea makes no sense." Which revision improves the tone for a group discussion?
{
~A. Your idea is wrong, and everyone can tell.#This keeps a disrespectful tone. Strong discussion language should address the idea without attacking the person.
=B. I am not sure I understand your idea yet. Could you explain your reasoning?#Correct. This revision keeps the discussion respectful, asks for clarification, and supports collaboration.
~C. I will ignore that idea and move on.#This shuts down communication. A better tone keeps the group focused while inviting explanation.
~D. That idea is confusing, so you should not talk.#This attacks the speaker and discourages participation. Respectful tone helps group members continue learning and contributing.
}
